<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pahang Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1770 CE – present (approx.) · capital Pekan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Largest state of the Malay Peninsula, held by the Bendahara family of Johor before becoming a sultanate in 1882.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pahang had been a dependency of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a> since the 17th century. Around 1770 it was granted as a hereditary fief to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> Bendahara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-abdul-majid">Tun Abdul Majid</a>, whose descendants ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekan">Pekan</a> as Raja Bendahara. The breakup of the Johor-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> realm in the early 19th century left them effectively independent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pahang was thinly populated, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> villages, gold workings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> along its long rivers and great forests inland. A civil war from 1857 to 1863 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-muadzam-shah-of-pahang">Wan Ahmad</a> to power, and in 1882 he proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ahmad Muadzam Shah. Chiefs ruled their districts with wide autonomy.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The British forced a resident on Pahang in 1888, provoking the Pahang uprising of 1891 to 1895 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dato-bahaman">Dato Bahaman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mat-kilau">Mat Kilau</a>. Pahang then joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-of-pahang">Abdullah</a> served as king from 2019 to 2024.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pahang-sultanate.jpg" alt="Pahang Sultanate" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Sanson, Nicolas (1600-1667). Cartographe Idrīsī, Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Šarīf Abū ʿAbd Allâh al- (1100?-1165?). Aut... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(139_of_160)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1770 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pekan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tun Abdul Majid, Ahmad Muadzam Shah, Abdullah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pahang Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pahang, Bendahara Kingdom of Pahang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=pahang-sultanate" aria-label="Pahang Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7550 2047 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pahang Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7870 2303L7881 2378M7870 2303L4997 651M7870 2303L7823 2316M7870 2303L7823 2316M7870 2303L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7881 2378h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7870 2303h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7870 2303h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pekan. <a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=pahang-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
